# A theremin synth

## Overview

Turn the screen into a theremin: slide a finger left and right to change
the pitch, up and down to change the volume. The `< sine >` arrows in
the top bar, or the volume buttons, switch between 10 instruments, from
a plain sine to a bass, a bell and three drums.

Three parts work together:

* `Synth`, a process that computes the sound of 5 voices sample by
  sample and streams the mix into one long-running `aplay`,
* `Sequencer`, which records what you play into 4 tracks and plays them
  back on voices 1 to 4,
* a Scenic scene that turns touches into `Synth.set(:live, frequency, volume)`
  and draws the sequencer's buttons.

This builds on the [Audio](10_audio.livemd) and
[Screen, touch and buttons](11_screen_touch_buttons.livemd) notebooks.

## Route audio to the loudspeaker

Already done at boot on a Fairphone 3+. Run it if you switched to the
earpiece in the Audio notebook.

```elixir
for {name, value} <- [
      {"Earpiece Switch", :off},
      {"SLIMBUS_0_RX Audio Mixer MultiMedia1", :off},
      {"QUIN_MI2S_RX Audio Mixer MultiMedia1", :on},
      {"Speaker Switch", :on}
    ],
    do: {name, ExAudio.cset(name, value)}
```

## The synth

`aplay` reads raw 16-bit samples from its standard input and plays them
as they arrive, so no WAV files are involved. Every 5 ms the synth
works out how many samples the speaker will need by 30 ms from now and
writes exactly those. That keeps the delay between touch and sound
short, and the sound never falls behind.

Frequency and volume slide from their old to their new value across
each chunk, and the waveform's phase carries over between chunks.
Without that, every finger movement would click.

The synth has 5 voices: `:live` for the pad and `1` to `4` for the
sequencer's tracks. Each chunk adds the sounding voices up sample by
sample in one pass and skips silent ones, so an idle track costs almost
nothing.

Each voice plays an instrument: a sound source, an envelope and
sometimes a filter. The envelope shapes every note: it rises during the
attack, falls during the decay to the sustain level while you hold on,
and fades out during the release after you lift your finger. Drums are
one-shot: a tap plays the whole hit however short it is. The table of
instruments is `@instruments`; `Synth.instrument(voice, name)` picks
one, heard from the voice's next note.

```elixir
defmodule Synth do
  use GenServer

  @rate 48_000
  @tick_ms 5
  # How far ahead of the speaker we write: lower is snappier, higher survives hiccups.
  @lead div(@rate * 30, 1000)
  # The pad plays on :live, the sequencer's tracks on 1 to 4.
  @voices [:live, 1, 2, 3, 4]

  # Each instrument: sound source, loudness, envelope times in seconds (attack, decay, release),
  # sustain level, whether it ignores note off, and the second oscillator's pitch ratio.
  @instruments [
    sine: %{source: :sine, level: 1.0, a: 0.005, d: 0.0, s: 1.0, r: 0.05, one_shot: false, ratio: 0.0},
    square: %{source: :square, level: 0.4, a: 0.005, d: 0.0, s: 1.0, r: 0.05, one_shot: false, ratio: 0.0},
    saw: %{source: :saw, level: 0.6, a: 0.005, d: 0.0, s: 1.0, r: 0.05, one_shot: false, ratio: 0.0},
    bass: %{source: :bass, level: 0.6, a: 0.005, d: 0.25, s: 0.3, r: 0.1, one_shot: false, ratio: 0.5},
    pluck: %{source: :triangle, level: 0.8, a: 0.002, d: 0.4, s: 0.0, r: 0.1, one_shot: false, ratio: 0.0},
    bell: %{source: :fm, level: 0.8, a: 0.002, d: 1.5, s: 0.0, r: 1.0, one_shot: false, ratio: 3.5},
    pad: %{source: :square, level: 0.4, a: 0.4, d: 0.0, s: 1.0, r: 0.6, one_shot: false, ratio: 0.0},
    kick: %{source: :kick, level: 1.0, a: 0.001, d: 0.3, s: 0.0, r: 0.0, one_shot: true, ratio: 0.0},
    snare: %{source: :snare, level: 0.8, a: 0.001, d: 0.15, s: 0.0, r: 0.0, one_shot: true, ratio: 0.0},
    hihat: %{source: :hihat, level: 0.8, a: 0.001, d: 0.05, s: 0.0, r: 0.0, one_shot: true, ratio: 0.0}
  ]
  @names Keyword.keys(@instruments)
  # The kick's pitch sweep falls to 1 % in 0.1 s.
  @sweep_factor 0.01 ** (1 / (0.1 * @rate))
  @two_pi 2 * :math.pi()

  def start, do: GenServer.start(__MODULE__, nil, name: __MODULE__)
  def stop, do: GenServer.stop(__MODULE__)

  @doc "The instrument names, in the order the pad's picker shows them."
  def instruments, do: @names

  @doc "Play `freq` Hz at volume `amp` (0.0 to 1.0) on `voice`. Volume 0 is note off."
  def set(voice, freq, amp) when voice in @voices, do: GenServer.cast(__MODULE__, {:set, voice, freq, amp |> max(0.0) |> min(1.0)})
  def off(voice) when voice in @voices, do: GenServer.cast(__MODULE__, {:off, voice})
  @doc "The instrument `voice` uses from its next note on."
  def instrument(voice, name) when voice in @voices and name in @names, do: GenServer.cast(__MODULE__, {:instrument, voice, name})

  def voice do
    %{
      freq: 440.0,
      amp: 0.0,
      cur_freq: 440.0,
      cur_amp: 0.0,
      phase: 0.0,
      phase2: 0.0,
      inst: :sine,
      next_inst: :sine,
      gate: false,
      stage: :idle,
      env: 0.0,
      sweep: 0.0,
      low: 0.0,
      band: 0.0,
      noise: 1,
      pending: nil
    }
  end

  def initial, do: Map.new(@voices, &{&1, voice()})

  @doc """
  Applies a `set/3` to one voice's state. Volume above 0 on a voice that isn't gated is a note on:
  the attack starts from the current envelope level, with the instrument chosen last. Volume 0 is a
  note off: the release starts, except for one-shot instruments, which play their decay to the end.
  """
  # A new instrument on a voice that still rings: the old sound fades out over one chunk first
  # (stage :fade), then the note starts. Switching sources mid-sound would click.
  def note(v, freq, amp) when amp > 0 and not v.gate and v.stage != :idle and v.next_inst != v.inst,
    do: %{v | gate: true, stage: :fade, pending: {freq, amp}}

  def note(v, freq, amp) when amp > 0 and not v.gate do
    fresh = v.stage == :idle
    inst = v.next_inst

    %{
      v
      | freq: freq,
        amp: amp,
        cur_freq: if(fresh, do: freq, else: v.cur_freq),
        cur_amp: if(fresh, do: amp, else: v.cur_amp),
        inst: inst,
        gate: true,
        stage: :attack,
        sweep: if(inst == :kick, do: 1.0, else: 0.0)
    }
  end

  def note(%{stage: :fade} = v, freq, amp) when amp > 0, do: %{v | pending: {freq, amp}}
  def note(v, freq, amp) when amp > 0, do: %{v | freq: freq, amp: amp}

  def note(v, _freq, _amp) do
    cond do
      # Released during the fade: the pending note still plays (a quick drum tap), then gets its note off.
      v.stage == :fade or @instruments[v.inst].one_shot or v.stage == :idle -> %{v | gate: false}
      true -> %{v | gate: false, stage: :release}
    end
  end

  @doc "Renders `n` samples of all voices mixed, as S16_LE, and returns them with the updated voices."
  # Two ticks can land in the same sample after a stall; the ramps below would divide by zero.
  def render(voices, 0), do: {<<>>, voices}

  def render(voices, n) do
    # Idle voices cost nothing. The envelope and the kick's pitch sweep move once per chunk and glide
    # across it, like volume and pitch. Each sounding voice becomes a tuple advanced once per sample.
    chunk =
      for {id, v} <- voices, v.stage != :idle, into: %{} do
        i = @instruments[v.inst]
        {stage, env} = envelope(i, v.stage, v.env, n)
        sweep = v.sweep * @sweep_factor ** n
        gain = v.cur_amp * v.env * i.level
        dp = v.cur_freq * (1 + 2 * v.sweep) / @rate
        dp_end = v.freq * (1 + 2 * sweep) / @rate
        k = {i.source, i.ratio, filter(i.source, (v.env + env) / 2), (dp_end - dp) / n, (v.amp * env * i.level - gain) / n, id}
        {id, {%{stage: stage, env: env, sweep: sweep}, {k, v.phase, dp, v.phase2, gain, v.low, v.band, v.noise}}}
      end

    {samples, oscs} = mix(Enum.map(chunk, fn {_id, {_, osc}} -> osc end), n, [])

    voices =
      Enum.reduce(oscs, voices, fn {k, phase, _dp, phase2, _gain, low, band, noise}, voices ->
        id = elem(k, 5)
        {env_state, _} = chunk[id]
        Map.update!(voices, id, &Map.merge(&1, Map.merge(env_state, %{phase: phase, phase2: phase2, low: low, band: band, noise: noise})))
      end)

    voices = Map.new(voices, fn {id, v} -> {id, v |> Map.merge(%{cur_freq: v.freq, cur_amp: v.amp}) |> start_pending()} end)
    {IO.iodata_to_binary(samples), voices}
  end

  # The envelope after `n` more samples: {stage, level}. Decay and release are exponential and reach
  # 1 % of their starting distance at the instrument's stated time.
  defp envelope(i, :attack, env, n) do
    env = env + n / (i.a * @rate)
    if env >= 1.0, do: {:decay, 1.0}, else: {:attack, env}
  end

  defp envelope(i, :decay, env, n) do
    env = i.s + (env - i.s) * factor(i.d) ** n

    cond do
      env - i.s > 0.001 -> {:decay, env}
      i.s == 0 -> {:idle, 0.0}
      true -> {:sustain, i.s}
    end
  end

  defp envelope(i, :release, env, n) do
    env = env * factor(i.r) ** n
    if env > 0.0005, do: {:release, env}, else: {:idle, 0.0}
  end

  defp envelope(_i, :fade, _env, _n), do: {:idle, 0.0}
  defp envelope(_i, stage, env, _n), do: {stage, env}

  # After a fade: start the note that waited, and release it at once if the finger already lifted.
  defp start_pending(%{pending: {freq, amp}, stage: :idle, gate: gate} = v) do
    v = note(%{v | pending: nil, gate: false}, freq, amp)
    if gate, do: v, else: note(v, freq, 0)
  end

  defp start_pending(v), do: v

  defp factor(+0.0), do: 0.0
  defp factor(seconds), do: 0.01 ** (1 / (seconds * @rate))

  # Filter coefficients (Chamberlin state-variable filter): the bass's cutoff follows the envelope.
  defp filter(:bass, env), do: @two_pi * (200 + 2500 * env) / @rate
  defp filter(:hihat, _env), do: 2 * :math.sin(:math.pi() * 7000 / @rate)
  defp filter(_source, _env), do: 0.0

  # ponytail: one pass in plain Elixir; Nx or a NIF if more voices or heavier instruments are needed.
  defp mix(oscs, 0, acc), do: {Enum.reverse(acc), oscs}

  defp mix(oscs, left, acc) do
    {sum, oscs} = step(oscs, 0.0, [])
    sample = round((sum |> max(-1.0) |> min(1.0)) * 32_767)
    mix(oscs, left - 1, [<<sample::signed-little-16>> | acc])
  end

  # Each source computes its sample and only the state it needs, then hands on to the next voice.
  defp step([], sum, oscs), do: {sum, oscs}
  defp step([{k, _, _, _, _, _, _, _} = osc | rest], sum, oscs), do: voice(elem(k, 0), osc, rest, sum, oscs)

  defp voice(:sine, {_, phase, _, _, gain, _, _, _} = o, rest, sum, oscs),
    do: step(rest, sum + gain * :math.sin(@two_pi * phase), [move(o) | oscs])

  defp voice(:kick, {_, phase, _, _, gain, _, _, _} = o, rest, sum, oscs),
    do: step(rest, sum + gain * :math.sin(@two_pi * phase), [move(o) | oscs])

  defp voice(:square, {_, phase, _, _, gain, _, _, _} = o, rest, sum, oscs), do: step(rest, sum + gain * square(phase), [move(o) | oscs])
  defp voice(:saw, {_, phase, _, _, gain, _, _, _} = o, rest, sum, oscs), do: step(rest, sum + gain * (2 * phase - 1), [move(o) | oscs])

  defp voice(:triangle, {_, phase, _, _, gain, _, _, _} = o, rest, sum, oscs),
    do: step(rest, sum + gain * triangle(phase), [move(o) | oscs])

  # Bell: FM, a sine whose phase is pushed around by a second sine; the push fades with the envelope.
  defp voice(:fm, {{_, ratio, _, _, _, _} = k, phase, dp, phase2, gain, low, band, noise}, rest, sum, oscs) do
    out = :math.sin(@two_pi * phase + 2 * gain * :math.sin(@two_pi * phase2))
    step(rest, sum + gain * out, [move({k, phase, dp, wrap(phase2 + dp * ratio), gain, low, band, noise}) | oscs])
  end

  # Bass: saw plus a square one octave down, low-passed.
  defp voice(:bass, {{_, ratio, f, _, _, _} = k, phase, dp, phase2, gain, low, band, noise}, rest, sum, oscs) do
    low = low + f * band
    band = f * (2 * phase - 1 + 0.5 * square(phase2) - low - 0.5 * band) + band
    step(rest, sum + gain * low, [move({k, phase, dp, wrap(phase2 + dp * ratio), gain, low, band, noise}) | oscs])
  end

  defp voice(:snare, {k, phase, dp, phase2, gain, low, band, noise}, rest, sum, oscs) do
    noise = next_noise(noise)
    out = 0.6 * (noise / 8_388_608 - 1) + 0.4 * triangle(phase)
    step(rest, sum + gain * out, [move({k, phase, dp, phase2, gain, low, band, noise}) | oscs])
  end

  # Hi-hat: noise with the lows filtered out.
  defp voice(:hihat, {{_, _, f, _, _, _} = k, phase, dp, phase2, gain, low, band, noise}, rest, sum, oscs) do
    noise = next_noise(noise)
    low = low + f * band
    high = noise / 8_388_608 - 1 - low - band
    step(rest, sum + gain * high, [move({k, phase, dp, phase2, gain, low, f * high + band, noise}) | oscs])
  end

  # Advances phase, pitch and gain by one sample.
  defp move({{_, _, _, ddp, dgain, _id} = k, phase, dp, phase2, gain, low, band, noise}),
    do: {k, wrap(phase + dp), dp + ddp, phase2, gain + dgain, low, band, noise}

  defp wrap(phase), do: phase - trunc(phase)
  # A small linear congruential generator: deterministic noise, kept in the voice's state.
  defp next_noise(x), do: rem(x * 1_140_671_485 + 12_820_163, 16_777_216)

  defp square(phase), do: if(phase < 0.5, do: 1.0, else: -1.0)
  defp triangle(phase), do: 1 - 4 * abs(phase - 0.5)

  @impl GenServer
  def init(nil) do
    args = ~w(-q -D plughw:0,0 -t raw -f S16_LE -r #{@rate} -c 1 --buffer-time=60000 --period-time=10000 -)
    port = Port.open({:spawn_executable, System.find_executable("aplay")}, [:binary, :exit_status, args: args])
    :timer.send_interval(@tick_ms, :tick)
    {:ok, %{voices: initial(), port: port, start: now(), written: 0}}
  end

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_cast({:set, voice, freq, amp}, s), do: {:noreply, update_voice(s, voice, &note(&1, freq, amp))}
  def handle_cast({:off, voice}, s), do: {:noreply, update_voice(s, voice, &note(&1, &1.freq, 0.0))}
  def handle_cast({:instrument, voice, name}, s), do: {:noreply, update_voice(s, voice, &%{&1 | next_inst: name})}

  defp update_voice(s, voice, fun), do: %{s | voices: Map.update!(s.voices, voice, fun)}

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_info(:tick, s) do
    due = div((now() - s.start) * @rate, 1_000_000) + @lead
    # After a long pause (busy CPU), skip ahead instead of piling up delay.
    written = max(s.written, due - 2 * @lead)
    {chunk, voices} = render(s.voices, due - written)
    Port.command(s.port, chunk)
    {:noreply, %{s | voices: voices, written: due}}
  end

  def handle_info({port, {:exit_status, code}}, %{port: port} = s), do: {:stop, {:aplay_exited, code}, s}

  defp now, do: System.monotonic_time(:microsecond)
end
```

A quick check of `render/2`: chunks join without a jump, voices mix and
clamp, envelopes rise and fall, drums play out, and a new instrument
waits for the next note.

```elixir
# Check: Synth.render/2 joins chunks, mixes and clamps voices, and plays envelopes, drums and instrument changes.
silent = Synth.initial()
decode = fn bin -> for <<x::signed-little-16 <- bin>>, do: x end
# A voice already holding a note at full envelope, as if it had been playing a while.
held = fn inst, amp -> %{Synth.voice() | inst: inst, amp: amp, cur_amp: amp, gate: true, stage: :sustain, env: 1.0} end
# Renders `seconds` of one voice on :live in 5 ms chunks, like the running synth, and returns its samples and new state.
play = fn v, seconds ->
  Enum.reduce(1..round(seconds * 200)//1, {[], v}, fn _, {samples, v} ->
    {bin, voices} = Synth.render(%{silent | live: v}, 240)
    {samples ++ decode.(bin), voices.live}
  end)
end

# Two chunks of 100 samples join into exactly the same wave as one chunk of 200.
{a, s1} = Synth.render(%{silent | live: held.(:sine, 0.5)}, 100)
{b, _} = Synth.render(s1, 100)
{whole, _} = Synth.render(%{silent | live: held.(:sine, 0.5)}, 200)
true = byte_size(a) == 200
true = a <> b == whole

# Only idle voices: 100 samples of zero. A 0-sample chunk renders nothing.
{zeros, _} = Synth.render(silent, 100)
true = zeros == <<0::size(1600)>>
{<<>>, _} = Synth.render(%{silent | live: held.(:sine, 0.5)}, 0)

# Two voices mix to the sum of each alone (±1 for rounding).
{live, _} = Synth.render(%{silent | live: held.(:saw, 0.5)}, 100)
{track, _} = Synth.render(%{silent | 1 => held.(:square, 0.5)}, 100)
{both, _} = Synth.render(%{silent | :live => held.(:saw, 0.5), 1 => held.(:square, 0.5)}, 100)
sums = Enum.zip_with(decode.(live), decode.(track), &(&1 + &2))
true = Enum.zip(sums, decode.(both)) |> Enum.all?(fn {sum, mixed} -> abs(sum - mixed) <= 1 end)

# Each voice keeps its own state across an odd-length chunk: 101 samples at 440 Hz and at 220 Hz.
low = %{held.(:saw, 0.5) | freq: 220.0, cur_freq: 220.0}
{_, after_odd} = Synth.render(%{silent | :live => held.(:sine, 0.5), 1 => low}, 101)
true = abs(after_odd.live.phase - 101 * 440 / 48_000) < 1.0e-9
true = abs(after_odd[1].phase - 101 * 220 / 48_000) < 1.0e-9

# Five loud voices clamp to full scale instead of wrapping around.
{loud, _} = Synth.render(Map.new(silent, fn {id, _} -> {id, held.(:square, 1.0)} end), 10)
true = Enum.all?(decode.(loud), &(abs(&1) == 32_767))

# Envelope: a sine reaches full level after its 5 ms attack, and after note off it falls under 1 %
# within its 50 ms release, then goes idle and renders zeros.
{_, v} = play.(Synth.note(Synth.voice(), 440, 0.5), 0.006)
true = v.stage in [:decay, :sustain] and v.env >= 0.99
{_, v} = play.(Synth.note(v, 440, 0), 0.05)
true = v.env < 0.011
{_, v} = play.(v, 0.05)
:idle = v.stage
{tail, _} = play.(v, 0.01)
true = Enum.all?(tail, &(&1 == 0))

# One-shot: a kick released right after it starts still plays its decay (1 % at 0.3 s), then goes idle.
kick = %{Synth.voice() | next_inst: :kick} |> Synth.note(110, 0.5) |> Synth.note(110, 0)
{hit, v} = play.(kick, 0.1)
true = Enum.any?(Enum.drop(hit, 4_000), &(abs(&1) > 100)) and v.stage == :decay
{_, v} = play.(v, 0.4)
:idle = v.stage

# Retrigger: tapping a drum again while it still rings restarts its attack.
{_, ringing} = play.(kick, 0.05)
:attack = ringing |> Synth.note(110, 0.5) |> Map.fetch!(:stage)

# Instrument change: takes effect at the next note on, not in the middle of a note.
v = Synth.note(Synth.voice(), 440, 0.5)
v = %{v | next_inst: :bass}
{_, v} = play.(v, 0.01)
:sine = v.inst
# The next note starts after the old one faded out (one 5 ms chunk).
{_, v} = play.(v |> Synth.note(440, 0) |> Synth.note(220, 0.5), 0.01)
:bass = v.inst

# Switching instrument while the old note still rings fades the old one out first: no jump, no click.
bell = %{Synth.voice() | next_inst: :bell} |> Synth.note(110, 0.5)
{_, bell} = play.(bell, 0.2)
{before, ringing} = play.(Synth.note(bell, 110, 0), 0.05)
{after_switch, v} = play.(%{ringing | next_inst: :kick} |> Synth.note(110, 0.5), 0.02)
true = abs(hd(after_switch) - List.last(before)) < 500
:kick = v.inst
# A quick tap during that fade still plays the whole drum hit.
tap = %{ringing | next_inst: :kick} |> Synth.note(110, 0.5) |> Synth.note(110, 0)
{hit, v} = play.(tap, 0.05)
true = v.inst == :kick and v.stage == :decay and Enum.any?(Enum.drop(hit, 240), &(abs(&1) > 100))

# Noise and filters: hihat and bass make sound, and rendering the same state twice gives the same samples.
for inst <- [:hihat, :bass, :snare, :bell, :pluck, :pad] do
  v = Synth.note(%{Synth.voice() | next_inst: inst}, 220, 0.5)
  {first, _} = play.(v, 0.05)
  {again, _} = play.(v, 0.05)
  true = first == again and Enum.any?(first, &(abs(&1) > 100))
end
```

Start it and play a note for one second:

```elixir
{:ok, _} = Synth.start()
Synth.set(:live, 440, 0.3)
Process.sleep(1000)
Synth.off(:live)
```

## The sequencer

The sequencer has 4 tracks that share one loop length. A track stores
what you played as a list of `{time_ms, frequency, volume, instrument}`
events, not as sound. Every 10 ms it looks up, for each playing track,
the last event at or before the current loop position and sends it to
that track's voice in `Synth`, only when it changed.

Recording runs for exactly one loop from 0 s while the other tracks
with content play along, then stops by itself. A take replaces what was
on the track before.

```elixir
defmodule Sequencer do
  use GenServer

  @tick_ms 10
  @tracks [1, 2, 3, 4]

  def start, do: GenServer.start(__MODULE__, nil, name: __MODULE__)
  @doc "Stops the sequencer's process; fine to call when it isn't running."
  def shutdown, do: if(Process.whereis(__MODULE__), do: GenServer.stop(__MODULE__), else: :ok)

  @doc "Loop length for all tracks, 1 to 30 s. Returns :busy while playing or recording."
  def set_length(seconds), do: call({:set_length, seconds}, :not_running)
  @doc "Loops `ids` from 0 s; `[]` means every track with content. Returns :empty if that's none."
  def play(ids), do: call({:play, ids}, :not_running)
  @doc "Records one pass into track `id` while the other tracks with content play along."
  def record(id) when id in @tracks, do: call({:record, id}, :not_running)
  def stop, do: call(:stop, :not_running)
  def clear(ids), do: call({:clear, ids}, :not_running)
  @doc "The pad's current sound. Kept in the take while recording, ignored otherwise."
  def touch(freq, amp, instrument), do: GenServer.cast(__MODULE__, {:touch, freq, amp, instrument})
  def status, do: call(:status, %{mode: :idle, position_ms: 0, length_ms: 8_000, filled: [], sounding: []})

  # Without a running sequencer, calls return `fallback`, so the pad keeps working instead of crashing.
  defp call(msg, fallback), do: if(Process.whereis(__MODULE__), do: GenServer.call(__MODULE__, msg), else: fallback)

  @doc "The last event at or before `position` ms, or nil before the first one. `events` are sorted by time."
  # ponytail: linear scan per tick, fine for a few thousand events; keep a cursor per track if takes get long.
  def at(events, position), do: events |> Enum.take_while(fn {t, _, _, _} -> t <= position end) |> List.last()

  @impl GenServer
  def init(nil) do
    :timer.send_interval(@tick_ms, :tick)
    {:ok, %{length_ms: 8_000, tracks: Map.new(@tracks, &{&1, []}), mode: :idle, t0: 0, take: [], sent: %{}}}
  end

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_call({:set_length, seconds}, _from, %{mode: :idle} = s),
    do: {:reply, :ok, %{s | length_ms: 1_000 * (seconds |> max(1) |> min(30))}}

  def handle_call({:set_length, _seconds}, _from, s), do: {:reply, :busy, s}

  def handle_call({:play, ids}, _from, s) do
    case if(ids == [], do: filled(s), else: ids) do
      [] -> {:reply, :empty, s}
      ids -> {:reply, :ok, start(s, {:playing, ids})}
    end
  end

  def handle_call({:record, id}, _from, s), do: {:reply, :ok, start(s, {:recording, id, filled(s) -- [id]})}
  def handle_call(:stop, _from, s), do: {:reply, :ok, idle(s)}

  def handle_call({:clear, ids}, _from, s) do
    # Clearing the track being recorded also throws away the take.
    s =
      case s.mode do
        {:recording, id, _} -> if id in ids, do: idle(s), else: s
        _ -> s
      end

    {:reply, :ok, %{s | tracks: Enum.reduce(ids, s.tracks, &Map.put(&2, &1, []))}}
  end

  def handle_call(:status, _from, s) do
    # What the playing tracks sound like right now, so the pad can light their bands in each track's colours.
    sounding = for({id, {_t, freq, amp, _instrument}} <- s.sent, amp > 0, do: {id, freq, amp}) |> Enum.sort()
    {:reply, %{mode: s.mode, position_ms: position(s), length_ms: s.length_ms, filled: filled(s), sounding: sounding}, s}
  end

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_cast({:touch, freq, amp, instrument}, %{mode: {:recording, _, _}} = s),
    do: {:noreply, %{s | take: [{position(s), freq, amp, instrument} | s.take]}}

  def handle_cast({:touch, _freq, _amp, _instrument}, s), do: {:noreply, s}

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_info(:tick, %{mode: :idle} = s), do: {:noreply, s}

  def handle_info(:tick, %{mode: {:recording, id, _}} = s) do
    if position(s) >= s.length_ms do
      tracks = %{s.tracks | id => Enum.reverse(s.take)}
      {:noreply, %{idle(s) | tracks: tracks}}
    else
      {:noreply, replay(s)}
    end
  end

  def handle_info(:tick, s), do: {:noreply, replay(s)}

  # Sends each playing track's current event to its Synth voice, only when it changed.
  defp replay(s) do
    ids =
      case s.mode do
        {:playing, ids} -> ids
        {:recording, _id, ids} -> ids
      end

    pos = rem(position(s), s.length_ms)

    Enum.reduce(ids, s, fn id, s ->
      event = at(s.tracks[id], pos)
      if event != s.sent[id], do: voice(id, event)
      %{s | sent: Map.put(s.sent, id, event)}
    end)
  end

  defp voice(id, nil), do: Synth.off(id)

  defp voice(id, {_t, freq, amp, instrument}) do
    Synth.instrument(id, instrument)
    Synth.set(id, freq, amp)
  end

  defp start(s, mode), do: %{silence(s) | mode: mode, t0: now(), take: []}
  defp idle(s), do: %{silence(s) | mode: :idle, take: []}

  defp silence(s) do
    Enum.each(@tracks, &Synth.off/1)
    %{s | sent: %{}}
  end

  defp filled(s), do: for({id, events} <- s.tracks, events != [], do: id)

  defp position(%{mode: :idle}), do: 0
  defp position(%{mode: {:recording, _, _}} = s), do: now() - s.t0
  defp position(s), do: rem(now() - s.t0, s.length_ms)

  defp now, do: System.monotonic_time(:millisecond)
end
```

This check records a 1 s take, so it takes about 1.5 s. It stops a
running sequencer and its tracks; run the start cell below again
afterwards.

```elixir
# Check: Sequencer.at/2 finds the event in effect, and a short take records, plays, restarts and clears.
events = [{100, 220.0, 0.5, :sine}, {300, 440.0, 0.2, :saw}]
nil = Sequencer.at(events, 50)
{100, 220.0, 0.5, :sine} = Sequencer.at(events, 100)
{100, 220.0, 0.5, :sine} = Sequencer.at(events, 299)
{300, 440.0, 0.2, :saw} = Sequencer.at(events, 5_000)
nil = Sequencer.at([], 10)

:ok = Sequencer.shutdown()
{:ok, _} = Sequencer.start()

# Nothing recorded yet: PLAY does nothing.
:empty = Sequencer.play([])

# A one-pass take of 1 s into track 1 ends by itself.
:ok = Sequencer.set_length(1)
:ok = Sequencer.record(1)
Sequencer.touch(440.0, 0.3, :sine)
Process.sleep(200)
Sequencer.touch(440.0, 0.0, :sine)
Process.sleep(1_000)
%{mode: :idle, filled: [1], length_ms: 1_000} = Sequencer.status()

# PLAY with nothing selected plays every track with content; the length can't change meanwhile.
:ok = Sequencer.play([])
%{mode: {:playing, [1]}} = Sequencer.status()
# 50 ms in, track 1 sounds as recorded; its band can light up in track 1's colours.
Process.sleep(50)
%{sounding: [{1, 440.0, 0.3}]} = Sequencer.status()
:busy = Sequencer.set_length(5)

# REC while playing restarts in recording mode; STOP throws the unfinished take away.
:ok = Sequencer.record(2)
%{mode: {:recording, 2, [1]}} = Sequencer.status()
Sequencer.touch(300.0, 0.4, :saw)
:ok = Sequencer.stop()
%{mode: :idle, filled: [1]} = Sequencer.status()

# Clearing the track being recorded stops the recording and keeps nothing.
:ok = Sequencer.record(3)
Sequencer.touch(330.0, 0.4, :square)
:ok = Sequencer.clear([3])
%{mode: :idle, filled: [1]} = Sequencer.status()

# Clearing, and the length limits.
:ok = Sequencer.clear([1])
%{filled: []} = Sequencer.status()
:ok = Sequencer.set_length(99)
%{length_ms: 30_000} = Sequencer.status()
:ok = Sequencer.set_length(0)
%{length_ms: 1_000} = Sequencer.status()

# Without a running sequencer the pad keeps working: calls fall back instead of crashing.
:ok = Sequencer.shutdown()
%{mode: :idle, filled: [], sounding: []} = Sequencer.status()
:not_running = Sequencer.play([])
:ok = Sequencer.shutdown()
```

Start it:

```elixir
{:ok, _} = Sequencer.start()
```

## The pad

The screen spans 4 octaves, from A2 (110 Hz) on the left to A6
(1760 Hz) on the right. Each note gets a band of the same width, dark
for sharps like the black keys on a piano, and every white key is
labelled, with the Cs in white.
The pitch slides smoothly between them, so a note is only in tune in
the middle of its band. The top of the pad is loud and the bottom
is silent.

The bar across the top has two rows. The first shows the note and its
frequency, and `< sine >` arrows that step through the instruments. The
second runs the sequencer:

* `1` to `4` select tracks; a yellow dot means the track has a take.
* `REC` records one loop into the selected track. It needs exactly one
  selected track and turns red while recording.
* `PLAY` loops the selected tracks, or every track with a take when
  none is selected, until `STOP`.
* `-` and `+` set the loop length, 1 to 30 s, while nothing runs.
* `DEL` clears the selected tracks after a second tap within 2 s.
* The 8 cells on the right show where the loop is.

Touching the bar doesn't play. Bands light up for the tracks playing
back as well as for your finger: your finger in fire colours, each track
in its own gradient (blue, green, violet, grey), shown as the bar in its
button.

```elixir
defmodule Theremin.Scene do
  use Scenic.Scene
  alias Scenic.Graph
  import Scenic.Primitives

  @low 110
  @octaves 4
  @names ~w(A A# B C C# D D# E F F# G G#)
  # Height of the top bar. Row 1: the note and the instrument arrows. Row 2: the sequencer.
  @bar 320
  # Every button is a square this size.
  @button 130
  # Button colours.
  @idle {40, 48, 110}
  @dim {22, 26, 50}
  @selected {90, 110, 230}
  @red {200, 30, 30}
  @green {30, 150, 60}
  # Each track's gradient, loud → quiet. The loud colour is also the bar in its button.
  @track_colors %{
    1 => {{30, 60, 200}, {130, 220, 255}},
    2 => {{20, 140, 50}, {170, 240, 90}},
    3 => {{120, 40, 180}, {240, 140, 220}},
    4 => {{110, 110, 120}, {240, 240, 245}}
  }
  # Loop position cells.
  @cell_on {140, 170, 255}
  @cell_off {30, 36, 84}

  @impl Scenic.Scene
  def init(scene, _param, _opts) do
    # Landscape: everything is drawn on a turned canvas, with the volume buttons at the bottom.
    {screen_w, screen_h} = scene.viewport.size
    {width, height} = {screen_h, screen_w}

    graph =
      Graph.build(font: :roboto, font_size: 44)
      |> group(
        fn g ->
          g
          |> rect({width, height}, fill: {20, 24, 60})
          |> note_bands(width, height)
          |> rect({width, @bar}, fill: {10, 12, 30})
          |> text("Slide to play", id: :note, font_size: 80, fill: :white, translate: {40, 105})
          |> text("sine", id: :instrument, font_size: 64, fill: :white, text_align: :center, translate: {width - 320, 96})
          |> draw_buttons(width)
          |> text("8 s", id: :length, font_size: 56, fill: :white, text_align: :center, translate: {1410, 255})
          |> progress_cells()
          |> circle(60, id: :dot, fill: :yellow, hidden: true)
        end,
        rotate: -:math.pi() / 2,
        translate: {0, screen_h}
      )

    request_input(scene, [:cursor_button, :cursor_pos, :key])
    # Polls the sequencer, so the bar notices when a recording ends by itself.
    :timer.send_interval(100, :refresh)

    scene =
      assign(scene,
        graph: graph,
        size: {width, height},
        down: false,
        instrument: :sine,
        freq: 440.0,
        lit: nil,
        selected: MapSet.new(),
        delete_armed_at: nil,
        status: Sequencer.status(),
        view: nil
      )

    {:noreply, scene} = redraw(scene)
    {:ok, scene}
  end

  # One band per note, centred on its pitch: sharps dark like piano keys, naturals labelled.
  defp note_bands(graph, width, height) do
    notes = 12 * @octaves
    band = width / notes

    Enum.reduce(0..notes, graph, fn n, graph ->
      name = Enum.at(@names, Integer.mod(n, 12))
      x = band * (n - 0.5)
      sharp = String.ends_with?(name, "#")
      graph = rect(graph, {band, height - @bar}, id: {:band, n}, fill: dark(n), stroke: {1, {20, 24, 60}}, translate: {x, @bar})

      cond do
        sharp -> graph
        name == "C" -> label(graph, n, :white, {x + band / 2, height - 30})
        true -> label(graph, n, {150, 160, 230}, {x + band / 2, height - 30})
      end
    end)
  end

  # Unlit bands: sharps darker, like piano keys.
  defp dark(n), do: if(String.ends_with?(Enum.at(@names, Integer.mod(n, 12)), "#"), do: {12, 14, 36}, else: {30, 36, 84})

  # Fire gradient by volume: red when loud (top), through orange, to yellow when quiet (bottom).
  defp fire(t) do
    t = t |> max(0) |> min(1)
    {round(180 + 75 * min(2 * t, 1)), round(220 * t), 0}
  end

  # A track's gradient: its loud colour at t = 0, its quiet colour at t = 1.
  defp gradient(id, t) do
    t = t |> max(0) |> min(1)
    {{r1, g1, b1}, {r2, g2, b2}} = @track_colors[id]
    {round(r1 + (r2 - r1) * t), round(g1 + (g2 - g1) * t), round(b1 + (b2 - b1) * t)}
  end

  defp paint(graph, n, fill), do: Graph.modify(graph, {:band, n}, &Scenic.Primitive.put_style(&1, :fill, fill))

  defp unlight(graph, nil), do: graph
  defp unlight(graph, n), do: paint(graph, n, dark(n))

  defp label(graph, n, fill, pos),
    do: text(graph, note_name(@low * 2 ** (n / 12)), font_size: 22, fill: fill, text_align: :center, translate: pos)

  # Every button as {id, {x, y}, label}. The same list draws them and hit-tests touches.
  defp buttons(width) do
    tracks = for id <- 1..4, do: {{:track, id}, {track_x(id), 170}, "#{id}"}

    tracks ++
      [
        {:instrument_prev, {width - 600, 10}, "<"},
        {:instrument_next, {width - 170, 10}, ">"},
        {:rec, {700, 170}, "REC"},
        {:play, {850, 170}, "PLAY"},
        {:stop, {1000, 170}, "STOP"},
        {:shorter, {1200, 170}, "-"},
        {:longer, {1490, 170}, "+"},
        {:delete, {1680, 170}, "DEL"}
      ]
  end

  defp track_x(id), do: 40 + (id - 1) * 150

  defp draw_buttons(graph, width) do
    graph =
      Enum.reduce(buttons(width), graph, fn {id, {x, y}, label}, graph ->
        size = if String.length(label) == 1, do: 72, else: 40

        graph
        |> rect({@button, @button}, id: {:bg, id}, fill: @idle, stroke: {2, {90, 100, 180}}, translate: {x, y})
        |> text(label,
          id: {:label, id},
          font_size: size,
          fill: :white,
          text_align: :center,
          translate: {x + @button / 2, y + @button / 2 + size / 3}
        )
      end)

    # A yellow dot on a track button when the track has content, and a bar in the track's colour.
    Enum.reduce(1..4, graph, fn id, graph ->
      graph
      |> circle(10, id: {:filled, id}, fill: :yellow, hidden: true, translate: {track_x(id) + @button - 22, 192})
      |> rect({90, 10}, fill: elem(@track_colors[id], 0), translate: {track_x(id) + 20, 275})
    end)
  end

  # Loop position: 8 cells that fill up as the loop plays.
  defp progress_cells(graph) do
    Enum.reduce(0..7, graph, fn i, graph -> rect(graph, {30, 60}, id: {:cell, i}, fill: @cell_off, translate: {1840 + i * 35, 205}) end)
  end

  defp button_at(width, {x, y}) do
    Enum.find_value(buttons(width), fn {id, {bx, by}, _label} ->
      if x >= bx and x < bx + @button and y >= by and y < by + @button, do: id
    end)
  end

  @impl Scenic.Scene
  def handle_input({:cursor_button, {_button, 1, _mods, pos}}, _id, scene) do
    {width, _height} = scene.assigns.size
    {_x, y} = pos = canvas(scene, pos)

    cond do
      y >= @bar -> play(assign(scene, down: true), pos)
      id = button_at(width, pos) -> press(scene, id)
      # The rest of the bar doesn't play.
      true -> {:noreply, scene}
    end
  end

  def handle_input({:cursor_button, {_button, 0, _mods, _pos}}, _id, scene) do
    Synth.off(:live)
    # The release goes into the take too, so it has silence where you lifted your finger.
    if scene.assigns.down, do: Sequencer.touch(scene.assigns.freq, 0.0, scene.assigns.instrument)

    scene.assigns.graph
    |> unlight(scene.assigns.lit)
    |> echo(scene.assigns.view)
    |> Graph.modify(:dot, &circle(&1, 60, hidden: true))
    |> show(assign(scene, down: false, lit: nil))
  end

  def handle_input({:cursor_pos, pos}, _id, %{assigns: %{down: true}} = scene), do: play(scene, canvas(scene, pos))

  # Only the volume keys: every touch also arrives as a :btn_touch key event.
  def handle_input({:key, {:key_volumeup, 1, _mods}}, _id, scene), do: switch_instrument(scene, 1)
  def handle_input({:key, {:key_volumedown, 1, _mods}}, _id, scene), do: switch_instrument(scene, -1)

  def handle_input(_input, _id, scene), do: {:noreply, scene}

  @impl GenServer
  def handle_info(:refresh, scene), do: refresh(scene)

  defp press(scene, :instrument_prev), do: switch_instrument(scene, -1)
  defp press(scene, :instrument_next), do: switch_instrument(scene, 1)

  defp press(scene, {:track, id}) do
    selected = scene.assigns.selected
    selected = if id in selected, do: MapSet.delete(selected, id), else: MapSet.put(selected, id)
    redraw(assign(scene, selected: selected, delete_armed_at: nil))
  end

  defp press(scene, :rec) do
    # REC needs exactly one selected track.
    with [id] <- MapSet.to_list(scene.assigns.selected), do: Sequencer.record(id)
    refresh(scene)
  end

  defp press(scene, :play) do
    Sequencer.play(Enum.sort(scene.assigns.selected))
    refresh(scene)
  end

  defp press(scene, :stop) do
    Sequencer.stop()
    refresh(scene)
  end

  defp press(scene, :shorter), do: change_length(scene, -1)
  defp press(scene, :longer), do: change_length(scene, 1)

  # DEL needs a second tap within 2 s, and at least one selected track.
  defp press(%{assigns: %{selected: selected, delete_armed_at: armed_at}} = scene, :delete) do
    cond do
      MapSet.size(selected) == 0 ->
        {:noreply, scene}

      armed_at != nil and now() - armed_at <= 2_000 ->
        Sequencer.clear(Enum.sort(selected))
        refresh(assign(scene, delete_armed_at: nil))

      true ->
        redraw(assign(scene, delete_armed_at: now()))
    end
  end

  defp change_length(scene, step) do
    Sequencer.set_length(div(scene.assigns.status.length_ms, 1_000) + step)
    refresh(scene)
  end

  defp refresh(scene), do: redraw(assign(scene, status: Sequencer.status()))

  # Pushes a new graph only when what the bar shows has changed.
  defp redraw(scene) do
    view = view(scene.assigns)

    if view == scene.assigns.view do
      {:noreply, scene}
    else
      scene.assigns.graph |> apply_view(view, scene.assigns) |> show(assign(scene, view: view))
    end
  end

  # What the bar should show, from the selection and the sequencer's status.
  defp view(%{selected: selected, status: status, delete_armed_at: armed_at}) do
    armed = armed_at != nil and now() - armed_at <= 2_000
    idle = status.mode == :idle

    rec =
      cond do
        match?({:recording, _, _}, status.mode) -> @red
        MapSet.size(selected) == 1 -> @idle
        true -> @dim
      end

    delete =
      cond do
        armed -> @red
        MapSet.size(selected) == 0 -> @dim
        true -> @idle
      end

    length = if idle, do: @idle, else: @dim

    fills =
      Map.new(1..4, &{{:track, &1}, if(&1 in selected, do: @selected, else: @idle)})
      |> Map.merge(%{
        rec: rec,
        play: if(match?({:playing, _}, status.mode), do: @green, else: @idle),
        shorter: length,
        longer: length,
        delete: delete
      })

    %{
      fills: fills,
      filled: status.filled,
      lit: if(idle, do: 0, else: min(div(8 * status.position_ms, status.length_ms) + 1, 8)),
      length: "#{div(status.length_ms, 1_000)} s",
      # Bands lit by playback: note index => the track's colour for its volume. On a shared note, the higher track wins.
      echo: Map.new(status.sounding, fn {id, freq, amp} -> {round(12 * :math.log2(freq / @low)), gradient(id, 1 - amp / 0.5)} end),
      delete: if(armed, do: "again?", else: "DEL")
    }
  end

  defp apply_view(graph, view, %{view: old, lit: live}) do
    # Playback bands that went quiet go dark, unless the finger is on them.
    old_echo = if old, do: Map.keys(old.echo), else: []
    graph = Enum.reduce((old_echo -- Map.keys(view.echo)) -- [live], graph, &unlight(&2, &1))
    graph = echo(graph, view)

    graph =
      Enum.reduce(view.fills, graph, fn {id, fill}, graph ->
        Graph.modify(graph, {:bg, id}, &Scenic.Primitive.put_style(&1, :fill, fill))
      end)

    graph =
      Enum.reduce(1..4, graph, fn id, graph ->
        Graph.modify(graph, {:filled, id}, &Scenic.Primitive.put_style(&1, :hidden, id not in view.filled))
      end)

    graph =
      Enum.reduce(0..7, graph, fn i, graph ->
        Graph.modify(graph, {:cell, i}, &Scenic.Primitive.put_style(&1, :fill, if(i < view.lit, do: @cell_on, else: @cell_off)))
      end)

    graph
    |> Graph.modify({:label, :delete}, &text(&1, view.delete))
    |> Graph.modify(:length, &text(&1, view.length))
  end

  defp echo(graph, nil), do: graph
  defp echo(graph, view), do: Enum.reduce(view.echo, graph, fn {n, fill}, graph -> paint(graph, n, fill) end)

  # Step through Synth's instruments, wrapping around at both ends. Heard from the next touch on.
  defp switch_instrument(scene, step) do
    names = Synth.instruments()
    instrument = Enum.at(names, Integer.mod(Enum.find_index(names, &(&1 == scene.assigns.instrument)) + step, length(names)))
    Synth.instrument(:live, instrument)

    scene.assigns.graph
    |> Graph.modify(:instrument, &text(&1, "#{instrument}"))
    |> show(assign(scene, instrument: instrument))
  end

  # Touches arrive in portrait screen coordinates: turn them back into the canvas.
  defp canvas(%{assigns: %{size: {width, _height}}}, {screen_x, screen_y}), do: {width - screen_y, screen_x}

  defp play(scene, {x, y}) do
    {width, height} = scene.assigns.size
    # Dragging into the bar keeps playing at full volume.
    y = max(y, @bar)
    quiet = (y - @bar) / (height - @bar)
    freq = @low * 2 ** (@octaves * x / width)
    amp = 0.5 * (1 - quiet)
    Synth.set(:live, freq, amp)
    Sequencer.touch(freq, amp, scene.assigns.instrument)
    lit = round(12 * @octaves * x / width)

    # Recoloured on every move: the colour follows the volume.
    scene.assigns.graph
    |> then(&if(scene.assigns.lit == lit, do: &1, else: &1 |> unlight(scene.assigns.lit) |> echo(scene.assigns.view)))
    |> paint(lit, fire(quiet))
    |> Graph.modify(:dot, &circle(&1, 60, translate: {x, y}, hidden: false))
    |> Graph.modify(:note, &text(&1, "#{note_name(freq)}  #{round(freq)} Hz"))
    |> show(assign(scene, lit: lit, freq: freq))
  end

  # The nearest note, counted in semitones from A4 (440 Hz).
  defp note_name(freq) do
    n = round(12 * :math.log2(freq / 440))
    "#{Enum.at(@names, Integer.mod(n, 12))}#{4 + Integer.floor_div(n + 9, 12)}"
  end

  defp now, do: System.monotonic_time(:millisecond)

  defp show(graph, scene), do: {:noreply, scene |> assign(graph: graph) |> push_graph(graph)}
end
```

## Play

Detach the IEx console from the screen, then start the pad. The synth
and the sequencer have to be running (see above).

The screen is 1080 × 2160 in portrait, and the driver only draws in
portrait. So the scene draws on a 2160 × 1080 canvas and turns it a
quarter turn onto the screen, and turns each touch back the other way.
Hold the phone with the volume buttons at the bottom.

```elixir
fbcon =
  Path.wildcard("/sys/class/vtconsole/vtcon*")
  |> Enum.find(&(File.read!(Path.join(&1, "name")) =~ "frame buffer"))

File.write!(Path.join(fbcon, "bind"), "0")
File.write!("/sys/class/graphics/fb0/blank", "0")

{:ok, viewport} =
  Scenic.ViewPort.start(
    name: :fp3_screen,
    size: {1080, 2160},
    default_scene: Theremin.Scene,
    # cursor: true makes the driver report finger movement, not just touch and release.
    drivers: [[module: Scenic.Driver.Local, cursor: true]]
  )
```

Try these:

* Change `@low` or `@octaves` in the scene, run **Stop**, then run
  the scene and **Play** cells again.
* Add an instrument of your own to `@instruments` in `Synth`, for example a triangle with a slow attack.
* Record a bass line on track 1, then a melody on track 2 while it plays.
* Replace the volume with the phone's tilt from the
  [Sensors](03_sensors.livemd) notebook.

## Stop

```elixir
Scenic.ViewPort.stop(viewport)
Sequencer.shutdown()
Synth.stop()
File.write!(Path.join(fbcon, "bind"), "1")
```
